The 6th Commandment:


You shall not commit adultery!


The fact that there is another commandment, which says: "You shall not covet your neighbour's wife!" shows how little this sixth commandment means what the earthly law says about it.


"You shall not commit adultery" can also mean: "You shall not break the peace of a marriage!" Peace is of course also understood to mean harmony. At the same time, this determines what a marriage should be like in general; because where there is nothing to break or disturb, the commandment has no validity, which is not based on earthly opinions and regulations, but on the divine will.


A marriage is only possible where harmony and peace reign as a matter of course. Where one person only seeks to live for the other and to please him. One-sidedness and the so seductive, deadly boredom are completely and forever excluded from the outset, as is the dangerous longing for distraction or the illusion of not being understood! The murderous tools of any happiness!


These evils cannot arise in a proper marriage, where one really lives for the other, because not wanting to be understood and the longing for distraction are merely the consequences of a pronounced selfishness that seeks to live only for itself and not for the other!


However, in true soul love, mutual, happy self-giving is something quite natural, and in this way, the possibility of one party being neglected is completely ruled out. Provided that the educational level of the two parties is not too great a gap!


This is a condition required by the law of attraction of like kinds in the great universe, which must be fulfilled if happiness is to be complete.


But where peace and harmony cannot be found, marriage does not deserve to be called marriage; for then it is not marriage, but only a community of earth, which as such has no value before God and therefore cannot bring blessing in the sense that is to be expected from a true marriage.


The sixth commandment therefore requires that a real marriage according to God's will is a prerequisite! Any other marriage is not protected. But woe to him who dares to disturb a true marriage in any way! For the triumph that he thinks he will have here on earth awaits him in a completely different form on the subtle level! He would like to flee in horror when he has to cross over into the kingdom where the consequences of his actions await him.


It is adultery in the broadest sense even when an attempt is made to separate two people who are truly in love with each other, as parents often do when one or another of these earthly circumstances is not to their liking!


And woe to that woman, woe to that man, whether young or old, who out of envy or flirtation knowingly bring discord or even rift between such a couple!


The pure love between two people should be sanctified before everyone, should inspire reverence and respect, but not desire! For it is under the protection of God's will!


If a feeling of such impure desire tries to arise, then a person should turn around and look with clear eyes among those people who have not yet joined anyone spiritually.


If he searches with earnestness and patience, he will definitely find a person who suits him in the way God wants, with whom he will then be happy without first burdening himself with a guilt that can never bring or grant happiness!


The great mistake of these people is often that they try to follow an emotional pressure that is always weak at first, they hold it within themselves by force, nurture it with artificial fantasy, until it grows stronger and fills them with pain and drives them to sin!


Thousands of human spirits would not be lost if they would only always pay attention to the beginning , which, if not created by the calculation of the mind, arises merely from inhuman flirtation, which in turn has its origin in the pernicious customs of earthly family and, above all, social life! These are often the pure marriage markets, no cleaner than the blatant slave trade in the Orient! In this lies a breeding ground for the seeds of adultery.


You parents, beware that you do not fall into the guilt of adultery against your children through too intellectual calculation! Countless people are already entangled in it! It takes a lot for them to break free from it!


You children, be careful not to become disturbers of the peace between your parents, otherwise you too will be guilty of adultery! Think about that carefully. Otherwise you will make yourselves enemies of your God, and there is no such enemy who will not ultimately fall victim to destruction in unspeakable torments, without God lifting a finger!


You should never destroy the peace and harmony between two people. Hammer this into your head so that it always stands before the eye of your soul as a warning.


(Abd-ru-shin: The Ten Commandments of God / The Lord's Prayer, Publisher of the Gralsbotschaft Foundation Stuttgart)

